A provider whose value reads state updates what reads the context.

passContext threads a provider's value to the component under it as an
argument, and the inliner used to seed a hidden var from it: `__ctx_label` was
copied from `txt` once, and the button writing `txt` changed nothing on screen,
on every target that lowers contexts. A context cannot be assigned, so the var
is read-only, and a spliced body now reads the provider's expression where it
read the var. Reactivity then sees `txt`, and the text's updater follows.

The value is substituted only when it is safe to read again -- a call to
anything but a pure function keeps the var, evaluated once -- which is why the
i18n archives still hold `defaultLocale()` in one place.

deny * `__ctx_label` -- the spliced body reads the provider's value, not a copy of it

-- main.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

context #label("a")

component show ui.node {
    ui.text(value=label)
}

ui.window #home(title="t", href="/") {
    var txt = "b"

    ui.vbox {
        ui.button(text="flip", @click { txt = "c" })
        label(txt) {
            show()
        }
    }
}
